title: '[pallet-revive] Rollback receipt logs on frame revert'
doc:
- audience: Todo
  description: |-
    # Description

    Ethereum receipt logs are accumulated outside the frame-level storage
    transaction used for contract execution. As a result, a log emitted by a
    nested call can remain in the final Ethereum receipt and bloom when that
    frame later reverts, even though its storage changes and FRAME events are
    rolled back.

    This change checkpoints the Ethereum receipt state at frame entry and restores
    the receipt encoding and bloom when the frame rolls back.

    This also preserves nested rollback semantics: logs produced by successful
    descendant calls are discarded if an ancestor frame later reverts.

    ## Integration

    This is a behavioral correctness fix for `pallet-revive`'s Ethereum-compatible
    receipt construction.

    No storage migration, runtime configuration change, or public API change is
    required.

    Downstream runtimes using the updated pallet will construct Ethereum receipts,
    blooms, and receipt roots using only logs from execution frames that ultimately
    commit.

    ## Review Notes

    The receipt accumulator contains an append-only encoded log buffer and a logs
    bloom.

    At frame entry, the implementation checkpoints:

    - the current encoded receipt length;
    - the current logs bloom.

    If the frame commits, no additional action is required and its receipt changes
    remain.

    If the frame rolls back, the encoded receipt is truncated to the checkpointed
    length and the previous bloom is restored.

    The checkpoint is constant-size and does not clone the accumulated receipt.

    Because checkpoints follow execution-frame boundaries, nested behavior is
    preserved. For example, if a descendant call succeeds but one of its ancestor
    frames later reverts, the ancestor checkpoint also removes the descendant's
    logs.

    Event weight accounting is intentionally unchanged. Work charged while
    executing a reverted frame remains charged.

    Regression coverage includes:

    - a reverted child call followed by a successful parent;
    - a successful child call followed by a successful parent;
    - a successful descendant whose ancestor later reverts.

    The tests also verify storage rollback, surviving FRAME events, encoded receipt
    contents, receipt root, and block bloom.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
